      "productionPort": {
        "ok": true,
        "answerability": "answered",
        "answerText": "Summary\nCurrently, users with a Microsoft 365 Copilot license can use the agents.\n\nAuthoritative context\n[Microsoft documentation context ΓÇö definition]\nManage access to agents in SharePoint ΓÇ║ Manage who can access the agents ΓÇ║ Control user access through licensing\nDocument: Manage access to agents in SharePoint Heading Path: Manage access to agents in SharePoint -> Manage who can access the agents -> Control user access through licensing Currently, users with a Microsoft 365 Copilot license can use the agents. You can use the Microsoft 365 Copilot setup guide in the Microsoft 365 admin center to assign the required licenses to users. For more information, see Assign licenses to users in the Microsoft 365 admin center and Microsoft 365 Copilot requirements. Admins can choose to edit the service plans under the Copilot license to specifically allow or block users from using Copilot experiences on SharePoint. Under the license details page for Microsoft 365 Copilot on the Microsoft 365 admin center, admins can turn 'Microsoft 365 Copilot for SharePoint' on or off on a per-user basis. For example, a user could be allowed to use Microsoft 365 Copilot on Teams but not use any agents on SharePoint alone. However, this change also disables Copilot in OneDrive and on the SharePoint page authoring Copilot for that user.\nSource: Manage access to agents in SharePoint\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/manage-access-agents-in-sharepoint\n\nCaveats / limitations\nMissing authoritative domain coverage: sharepoint\n\nSources\nManage access to agents in SharePoint ΓÇö https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/manage-access-agents-in-sharepoint",
